WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.460
Those in the know already know

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:04.879
Pokemon is a shortened name
for the "Pokemon" games'

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:07.424
main characters,
pocket monsters.

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.259
The infamous "gotta
catch 'em all" catchphrase

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.052
is the anglicized
interpretation of

00:00:11.136 --> 00:00:14.681
the Japanese getto suru,
which more directly translates

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:17.600
to "getting," the main
objective of the game.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.437
Through open-world exploration,
trade, and battling,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.148
you play as a trainer
trying to collect, nurture,

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:26.151
and evolve as many
Pokemon as you can.

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:29.404
These fantastical creatures
exist as sentient beings

00:00:29.487 --> 00:00:32.574
somewhere between wild
animal and beloved pet.

00:00:32.657 --> 00:00:35.535
Monsters that are collected
in stored in Poke Balls,

00:00:35.618 --> 00:00:38.955
Inspired by gachapon balls,
round plastic containers

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:42.500
used to house small collectible
items in vending machines,

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:45.086
the contraptions serve
as storage containers

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.672
and presumably mini bio-domes.

00:00:47.756 --> 00:00:50.592
The Pokemon are inspired
by flora and fauna,

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:54.721
science, religion, yokai, and
other supernatural beings.

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:58.224
But the series is teaming with
not just Japanese folklore

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:00.977
but versions of monsters
from across the globe,

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.730
made cute with
softened features.

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:05.523
Quite a few
of the "Monstrum" monsters

00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:08.735
have Pokemon counterparts,
like the golem,

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:13.156
Yuki Onna, Godzilla,
Mothra, banshees,

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:16.743
hellhounds, monstrous
plants, and kasha.

00:01:16.826 --> 00:01:18.912
The bond between
Pokemon and trainer,

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:22.081
monster and human, is
one built on trust,

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:23.917
loyalty, and
reciprocal benefits.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.253
In this way, Pokemon
distinguish themselves

00:01:27.337 --> 00:01:28.922
from other monsters.

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:31.758
While there are monsters who
may show kindness to humans

00:01:31.841 --> 00:01:34.385
or provide protection,
they still use fear

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:37.347
if not in behavior,
at least in appearance.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.516
Nintendo didn't think
"Pokemon" would do well

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:42.435
with the Japanese audience,
let alone a global one.

00:01:42.519 --> 00:01:45.814
They didn't even put any
promotion behind the first game.

00:01:45.897 --> 00:01:47.398
So what changed?

00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:51.319
Why did pocket monsters
become so freakishly popular?

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:53.530
[dramatic music]

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:56.741
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:01:56.825 --> 00:01:58.701
"Pokemon" changed
the monster genre

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.496
by making monsters
cute, not scary,

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:05.625
capitalizing on already popular
monster consumer culture.

00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:07.877
The first "Pokemon" game
for Nintendo's Game Boy

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:12.048
launched in Japan in 1996,
with a 10-year-old protagonist

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:15.385
and 151 creatures to
discover, catch, train,

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:17.720
and battle against one another.

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:20.640
The "Pokemon" game would
become a global phenomenon,

00:02:20.723 --> 00:02:24.686
evolving into a card game,
multiple anime series, movies,

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:27.689
and a seemingly endless
barrage of consumer goods

00:02:27.772 --> 00:02:31.526
like plush toys, makeup,
clothing, and more games.

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:34.904
With Japan's intense focus
on education, long commutes,

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:38.074
and jam-packed schedules,
"Pokemon" offered children

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:40.910
a portable and
communal space for play

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:43.830
that was accessible
and pleasantly distracting.

00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:45.957
The game's inventor,
Satoshi Tajiri,

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.585
was mentored by one
of Nintendo's greats,

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:50.879
Shigeru Miyamoto,
video-game designer

00:02:50.962 --> 00:02:53.214
and inventor of Mario.

00:02:53.298 --> 00:02:54.966
Tajiri wove his own childhood

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:57.552
into part of
the "Pokemon" mythos.

00:02:57.635 --> 00:02:59.679
Spending much
of his childhood outside,

00:02:59.762 --> 00:03:00.847
Tajiri was an avid
collector of bugs,

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:04.309
tadpoles, and other
small creatures.

00:03:04.392 --> 00:03:05.685
Most kids are.

00:03:05.768 --> 00:03:07.854
I remember a time I brought
a frog into the house

00:03:07.937 --> 00:03:10.398
with a leach attached to it,
a two-for-one catch

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:12.191
I couldn't wait to show off.

00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:14.444
My mom was not so thrilled.

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:17.780
Come to think of it, that combo
might make a cool Pokemon.

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.658
We wouldn't have "Pokemon"
if it wasn't for Japan's

00:03:20.742 --> 00:03:23.620
dense, eclectic,
and vast folklore.

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:25.371
Influenced and inspired in part

00:03:25.455 --> 00:03:27.415
by polytheistic belief systems,

00:03:27.498 --> 00:03:29.584
the yokai tradition gives
us some of the world's

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:33.838
most unusual and unusually
specific monstrous creatures,

00:03:33.922 --> 00:03:36.841
and there is a long history
of collecting and profiting

00:03:36.925 --> 00:03:40.303
from depictions of these
strange and supernatural beings.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:44.057
Yokai-themed card games didn't
even begin with "Pokemon."

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.809
Hundreds of years before,
in the Edo period,

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:50.688
yokai-themed karuta, decorated
paper or cardboard cards,

00:03:50.772 --> 00:03:52.357
were all the rage.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.067
Towards the end of
the 19th century,

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.694
these cards were immensely
popular with children

00:03:56.778 --> 00:03:59.072
who collected the
game cards with vigor.

00:03:59.155 --> 00:04:01.616
In part because of the boom
of yokai consumer goods

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:04.369
like these, 19th-century
Japanese philosopher

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:07.747
Enryo Inoue was afraid
the deep folkloric history

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.291
of the strange creatures
would prevent the success

00:04:10.375 --> 00:04:14.337
of industrialism and hinder
Japan's journey to modernity,

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.591
so he invented anthropological
system called Yokaigaku

00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:21.177
that cataloged
and debunked yokai stories.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.096
Ironically,
his detailed accounts

00:04:23.179 --> 00:04:26.015
have helped preserve yokai lore.

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:29.769
19th-century Japan also
produced the first manga comics.

00:04:29.852 --> 00:04:32.480
Prolific artists
Hokusai is credited

00:04:32.563 --> 00:04:34.691
with naming this
particular style of art--

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:37.318
entertaining, whimsical
pictures that honored

00:04:37.402 --> 00:04:40.154
existing Japanese
illustration traditions

00:04:40.238 --> 00:04:43.366
while incorporating European
style-cartooning elements.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:46.077
Respected manga
artist Shigeru Mizuki

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.539
is credited with reviving
popular interest in yokai.

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:52.458
In 1960, his series
"Hakaba Kitaro,"

00:04:52.542 --> 00:04:55.420
which follows a young boy's
encounters with various yokai,

00:04:55.503 --> 00:04:57.797
was deemed
too scary for children,

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:01.217
but of course, that did
not stop its popularity.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.969
The playful plots
promoted harmony

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:04.721
between humans and yokai,

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:07.724
a message with a long
tradition in Japan.

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:10.601
It was also in the
20th century that another part

00:05:10.685 --> 00:05:14.522
of "Pokemon's" unique
heritage was born: kaiju.

00:05:14.605 --> 00:05:18.026
The giant monster film genre
began in 1954 with "Gojira,"

00:05:18.109 --> 00:05:20.653
you can learn more
about that here,

00:05:20.737 --> 00:05:22.697
and was immediately popular,

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:26.200
remaining a mainstay of
Japanese cinema to this day.

00:05:26.284 --> 00:05:28.536
Saburo Ishizuki
produced the first

00:05:28.619 --> 00:05:31.664
vinyl collectible
kaiju figurines,

00:05:31.748 --> 00:05:34.292
notably cuter than their
onscreen predecessors.

00:05:34.375 --> 00:05:36.085
They merged the
cute with the scary

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:39.088
and created tiny consumers
hellbent on holding

00:05:39.172 --> 00:05:42.091
their favorite characters
in their own hands.

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:45.178
Seeing a kaiju battle
onscreen was cool enough,

00:05:45.261 --> 00:05:47.513
but getting the chance
to recreate that scene

00:05:47.597 --> 00:05:51.309
or better yet, create your
very own with your friends?

00:05:51.392 --> 00:05:53.478
Now that is epic.

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:56.522
Illustrated Kaiju guidebooks
which I can't help but see

00:05:56.606 --> 00:05:59.192
as the modern homage
to medieval bestiaries,

00:05:59.275 --> 00:06:02.737
gave more details and background
stories to the characters.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:03.863
"Pokemon" would adopt
something similar

00:06:03.946 --> 00:06:07.492
with the Pokedex,
a digital bestiary.

00:06:07.575 --> 00:06:10.620
This brings us to the tech
side of "Pokemon" lore.

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:12.497
Matt Alt author of
"Pure Invention:

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.457
How Japan Made
the Modern World,"

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.542
argues that the
success of the game

00:06:16.626 --> 00:06:19.378
comes as a result of decades
of positive marketing

00:06:19.462 --> 00:06:21.923
of influential toys and
consumer electronics

00:06:22.006 --> 00:06:25.760
to the American market
that began in the 1950s.

00:06:25.843 --> 00:06:28.096
"Pac-Man" took video
games from the arcade

00:06:28.179 --> 00:06:31.641
into the supermarkets,
schools, and homes in 1980.

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.851
Then in 1989,
the release of Game Boy

00:06:33.935 --> 00:06:37.396
meant that anyone anywhere
could play video games,

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:39.732
a massive market shift.
Nintendo was ready

00:06:39.816 --> 00:06:41.943
to dominate and exploit.

00:06:42.026 --> 00:06:44.821
Nintendo launched Tajiri's
relatively simple "Pokemon" game

00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:47.657
in 1996 when they
were giving up hope

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:48.825
on the longevity
of the Game Boy.

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:51.661
The game was produced
with an unusual

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:54.080
and frankly genius feature.

00:06:54.163 --> 00:06:55.665
Released in two cartridges,

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:57.667
"Pokemon Red"
and "Pokemon Green,"

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.877
you had to trade with another
player to collect them all

00:06:59.961 --> 00:07:04.841
since 11 of the original
151 pocket monsters

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:07.468
would only evolve after trading,

00:07:07.552 --> 00:07:09.804
an intentional design
choice by Tajiri,

00:07:09.887 --> 00:07:11.597
who aimed to promote
social connection

00:07:11.681 --> 00:07:13.224
in the real world.

00:07:13.307 --> 00:07:15.601
It was this game design
strategy that continues

00:07:15.685 --> 00:07:17.645
to work for the franchise today.

00:07:17.728 --> 00:07:20.356
Each iteration of the game
contains unique Pokemon

00:07:20.439 --> 00:07:23.192
and badges that can only
be collected by playing

00:07:23.276 --> 00:07:26.028
and purchasing both
versions of the release.

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:28.281
Hell, I have "Pokemon Diamond"
and "Pokemon Pearl"

00:07:28.364 --> 00:07:30.700
sitting in my Switch
carrier right now.

00:07:30.783 --> 00:07:33.452
As "Pokemon" sales
began to climb in Japan,

00:07:33.536 --> 00:07:36.914
Nintendo decided to capitalize
on the growing popularity.

00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:39.292
A manga comic featuring
the cute monsters

00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:41.169
was quickly produced
and published

00:07:41.252 --> 00:07:44.338
in one of the most popular
magazines for young boys.

00:07:44.422 --> 00:07:47.258
An anime followed
the next spring in 1997,

00:07:47.341 --> 00:07:49.385
and then the first
big screen film.

00:07:49.468 --> 00:07:51.387
It was virtually
impossible to escape

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.723
the game's presence
in Japanese culture,

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:56.142
and its popularity
kept expanding.

00:07:56.225 --> 00:07:57.977
The game and
its various products

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:01.022
were released
in East Asia in 1997.

00:08:01.105 --> 00:08:03.608
Part of the franchise's success
was its ability to appeal

00:08:03.691 --> 00:08:06.527
to all genders
and a far wider age range

00:08:06.611 --> 00:08:08.404
than previously
believed possible.

00:08:08.487 --> 00:08:10.615
And a big part of that appeal?

00:08:10.698 --> 00:08:11.824
Pikachu.

00:08:11.908 --> 00:08:14.493
While many Pokemon
are undeniably cute,

00:08:14.577 --> 00:08:18.539
with large eyes, expressive
faces, and exaggerated features,

00:08:18.623 --> 00:08:21.417
they create strong emotional
attachments with consumers

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:24.879
when placed in narratives
in the anime and manga,

00:08:24.962 --> 00:08:27.840
contributing to their
perception as kawaii.

00:08:27.924 --> 00:08:30.426
Kawaii means more
than just cute.

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:32.345
It's really a cultural
touchstone

00:08:32.428 --> 00:08:34.430
that began in the 1970s.

00:08:34.514 --> 00:08:37.516
Something is kawaii only
if its cute, soft, round,

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.602
childlike features
are complimented

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:42.355
by their sweet and
gentle attributes.

00:08:42.438 --> 00:08:45.358
One of the anime's
creators, Masakazu Kubo,

00:08:45.441 --> 00:08:48.152
has said that the original
anime's target demographic

00:08:48.236 --> 00:08:50.446
was young girls and mothers.

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:53.115
Remember, the original
Nintendo game's graphics

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:55.743
were far simpler than
what they are today.

00:08:55.826 --> 00:08:58.412
It's difficult to show
cute in eight bit,

00:08:58.496 --> 00:09:01.249
so which Pokemon could make the
leap from the console screen

00:09:01.332 --> 00:09:03.167
and into the hearts
of the massive?

00:09:03.251 --> 00:09:04.710
It's Pikachu.

00:09:04.794 --> 00:09:07.546
Chosen for its bright,
non-aggressive color,

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:10.258
easy-to-say name,
sweet, expressive face,

00:09:10.341 --> 00:09:12.551
and memorable,
cuddly silhouette,

00:09:12.635 --> 00:09:14.512
Pikachu became a global icon

00:09:14.595 --> 00:09:16.889
akin to Mickey Mouse
or Hello Kitty.

00:09:16.973 --> 00:09:20.559
"Pokemon" first appeared to
American audiences in 1998,

00:09:20.643 --> 00:09:23.646
as "Pokemon Red version"
and "Pokemon Blue version."

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:28.109
By 1999, the series
had earned $5 billion.

00:09:28.192 --> 00:09:31.279
Kids across the world
couldn't get enough.

00:09:31.362 --> 00:09:32.780
I was one of those kids.

00:09:32.863 --> 00:09:35.866
I collected the cards
religiously and meticulously,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:39.620
organizing my binder at school,
on the bus, and at home.

00:09:39.704 --> 00:09:41.455
I carried that thing
to my friend's houses

00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:42.665
where we would trade cards

00:09:42.748 --> 00:09:45.251
with no intention
of playing the game.

00:09:45.334 --> 00:09:47.169
For me, it wasn't
just about finding

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:50.381
an elusive holographic
or balancing my stats.

00:09:50.464 --> 00:09:51.799
I simply loved looking
at the pictures

00:09:51.882 --> 00:09:54.885
and learning
the monster's traits.

00:09:54.969 --> 00:09:57.596
I guess in a way, I never
stopped being that little girl.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.974
My monster collecting
just looks different now.

00:10:00.057 --> 00:10:02.768
Monsters aren't the only
core appeal of "Pokemon."

00:10:02.852 --> 00:10:05.479
It feeds that urge to
explore the outdoors

00:10:05.563 --> 00:10:08.232
at a scale few could
achieve in real life.

00:10:08.316 --> 00:10:09.942
It doesn't matter
if you were a kid

00:10:10.026 --> 00:10:11.027
in an apartment high-rise
or an adult

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:13.988
trapped in a subway
commuting to work.

00:10:14.071 --> 00:10:16.991
Rain or shine, come hell
or a dying battery life,

00:10:17.074 --> 00:10:19.201
it allows you to
organically experience

00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:22.747
a colorful, diverse natural
world, with the added bonus

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:25.791
of a seemingly unending
array of wild animals

00:10:25.875 --> 00:10:29.920
or better yet, monsters
you get to keep as pets.

00:10:30.004 --> 00:10:32.631
The protagonist of the first
"Pokemon" game and anime

00:10:32.715 --> 00:10:35.676
is named after the
game's creator, Satoshi.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.636
In English, he is called Ash.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:39.847
He and the Pokemon
professors, Oak, Elm, Birch,

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:43.225
Rowan, Juniper,
Sycamore, and Magnolia,

00:10:43.309 --> 00:10:45.603
emphasize the
importance of nature

00:10:45.686 --> 00:10:48.606
as an instructive,
respected tool.

00:10:48.689 --> 00:10:50.775
Viewed from an
environmentally friendly lens,

00:10:50.858 --> 00:10:53.152
the game teaches players
to respect nature

00:10:53.235 --> 00:10:55.488
and its inhabitants
as living creatures

00:10:55.571 --> 00:10:59.158
integral to biodiversity,
not simple commodities.

00:10:59.241 --> 00:11:02.203
Not all interpretations of
the game are so positive.

00:11:02.286 --> 00:11:04.288
Some naysayers and
critics will argue

00:11:04.372 --> 00:11:06.791
that Pokemon encourages
children to not only

00:11:06.874 --> 00:11:09.543
feel familiar with conflict,
but seek it out,

00:11:09.627 --> 00:11:12.046
citing the franchise's
battle elements.

00:11:12.129 --> 00:11:13.673
Others claim that
capturing and trading

00:11:13.756 --> 00:11:15.925
wild Pokemon animals
promotes hunting

00:11:16.008 --> 00:11:18.803
or the mistreatment of
nature, where value is placed

00:11:18.886 --> 00:11:21.722
on how they can work
for or fight for humans.

00:11:21.806 --> 00:11:24.392
The staunchest of critics
will even point out

00:11:24.475 --> 00:11:27.269
that trapping Pokemon, then
transporting them to trade,

00:11:27.353 --> 00:11:29.397
where they might end up
as laborers or fighters,

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:31.357
has some problematic connections

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:34.110
to colonial history and slavery.

00:11:34.193 --> 00:11:35.778
Debates about these
interpretations

00:11:35.861 --> 00:11:38.406
have been going on pretty
much since "Pokemon" emerged

00:11:38.489 --> 00:11:40.533
on the global marketplace.

00:11:40.616 --> 00:11:43.452
Despite controversy,
the game's lingering impact

00:11:43.536 --> 00:11:45.037
cannot be denied.

00:11:45.121 --> 00:11:47.790
There are now over
1,000 types of Pokemon,

00:11:47.873 --> 00:11:50.584
and the franchise is
stronger than ever.

00:11:50.668 --> 00:11:52.962
The launch of
"Pokemon Go" in 2016

00:11:53.045 --> 00:11:54.588
broke gaming barriers again.

00:11:54.672 --> 00:11:58.342
That became the most downloaded
smartphone game in history,

00:11:58.426 --> 00:12:01.804
reaching 21 million users only
two weeks after its launch

00:12:01.887 --> 00:12:06.600
in the U.S., with 40% of those
users over the age of 25.

00:12:06.684 --> 00:12:08.477
Tajiri's original
intent is honored

00:12:08.561 --> 00:12:10.396
with this next evolution,

00:12:10.479 --> 00:12:11.772
encouraging players to
interact with one another

00:12:11.856 --> 00:12:15.025
outside of the
insular gaming bubble.

00:12:15.109 --> 00:12:17.695
"Pokemon Go" is
a universal game you can play

00:12:17.778 --> 00:12:19.780
from your phone literally
anywhere in the world.

00:12:19.864 --> 00:12:23.200
It requires going
outside, moving around,

00:12:23.284 --> 00:12:25.244
and often interacting
with others,

00:12:25.327 --> 00:12:27.371
promoting physical
and mental health.

00:12:27.455 --> 00:12:30.291
I've always believed that
monsters have the potential

00:12:30.374 --> 00:12:32.751
to unite us, a way for us
to understand the lives

00:12:32.835 --> 00:12:36.338
of other people and
better know ourselves.

00:12:36.422 --> 00:12:39.550
While folklore and oral
storytelling began that trend,

00:12:39.633 --> 00:12:40.759
with each new
communication technology,

00:12:40.843 --> 00:12:43.596
we've long profited
from monsters,

00:12:43.679 --> 00:12:45.973
socially and commercially.

00:12:46.056 --> 00:12:47.683
"Pokemon's" heritage places it

00:12:47.766 --> 00:12:50.394
within the realm of
national identity,

00:12:50.478 --> 00:12:51.854
reflecting and influencing
Japan's cultural,

00:12:51.937 --> 00:12:55.900
religious, social,
and environmental ideals.

00:12:55.983 --> 00:12:58.527
Globalization widens
this worldview,

00:12:58.611 --> 00:13:00.154
introducing Japanese history

00:13:00.237 --> 00:13:03.699
into an unexpected
mainstream global context.

00:13:03.782 --> 00:13:06.994
"Pokemon" just further proves
what I've always known:

00:13:07.077 --> 00:13:09.622
monsters are meant to be shared.

00:13:09.705 --> 00:13:10.664
I wanted to catch it.

00:13:10.748 --> 00:13:12.374
No, 'cause now it's gone.

00:13:12.458 --> 00:13:14.752
The main objective of
the game-- [laughing]

00:13:14.835 --> 00:13:16.045
Pocket monsters.

00:13:17.213 --> 00:13:18.464
My eight-year-old self
would be so proud.

00:13:18.547 --> 00:13:19.340
Cut.

